[2026-04-21 pt3-results] model=claude-opus-4-7[1m]
PROMPT:
  [auto-continuation from Pt3 iteration intent above]
ACTION:
  Pt3 executed end-to-end. 300 Monte Carlo trials completed in ~17 min.
  Findings are SUBTLE and worth reading carefully.

  === VALIDATION WINDOW (2020-2022, where MC selection happened) ===

  Summary statistics across 300 trials:
                          mean    std      min     max
    s_trending            0.498   0.286   0.004   0.998
    s_volatile            0.733   0.455   0.007   1.499
    s_crash               0.251   0.142   0.001   0.499
    sortino               0.927   0.065   0.818   1.100
    sharpe                0.645   0.042   0.574   0.754
    cagr %                9.56    0.80    8.50    11.79
    max_dd %            -28.16    2.19  -32.89   -23.63

  Pt1 val Sortino = 0.8205. 299/300 MC trials beat it (99.7%).
  MC best val Sortino   = 1.1005.
  MC best sizing        = (s_trending=0.744, s_volatile=0.035, s_crash=0.490)

  Surprising pattern — correlation of each sizing component with Sortino
  across the 300 trials:
    s_volatile  vs  Sortino:  -0.727   (STRONG NEGATIVE)
    s_crash     vs  Sortino:  +0.612   (STRONG POSITIVE)
    s_trending  vs  Sortino:  +0.072   (near zero)

  TRANSLATION: On the 2020-2022 validation window, the data says:
    (a) Shorting MORE in volatile regimes HURTS    - opposite of Pt1 thesis
    (b) Shorting MORE in crash regimes HELPS       - opposite of Pt1 thesis
    (c) Short sizing in trending regimes is noise

  This directly overturns the "asymmetric sizing" rationale I wrote into
  Pt1: the assumption was that shorts pay off in high-dispersion (volatile)
  regimes and are dangerous (squeeze risk) during crashes. On val, the
  opposite was true.

  === TEST WINDOW (2022-2026, held out) ===

  But here's the catch. Applying the MC-winning sizing (0.744, 0.035, 0.490)
  to the test window UNDERPERFORMS Pt1:

                               Pt1 (hand-picked)   Pt3 (MC-selected)
    test sharpe                 0.6291              0.5465
    test sortino                0.9102              0.7947
    test cagr %                 7.31                6.43
    test max drawdown %        -14.00              -19.99         (worse)
    test long leg %           +63.83              +61.44
    test short leg %          -10.14               -9.36

  Pt1's hand-picked sizing BEATS its own MC replacement on the held-out
  test set, despite losing 299/300 to MC on validation. This is a classic
  "winning the validation Olympics" failure — the MC optimum overfit to
  val-period regime structure and didn't generalize.

  Why? 2020-2022 contained COVID crash, tech rally, 2022 bear onset.
  2022-2026 is mostly bull + AI rotation. Short-leg sizing that worked
  when "crashes keep going down" (val) backfired when "crashes bounce
  back quickly" (test).

  === ICM IMPLICATIONS (the actual payoff of this experiment) ===

  Pt3 produces THREE conclusions worth including in the memo:

  1. Pt1's hand-picked sizing is a coincidence winner on test, not a
     principled winner. 299/300 random configurations beat it on val.
     It generalizes better to 2022-2026 than the optimal val config,
     but there's no reason to believe that's durable.

  2. The asymmetric-sizing thesis (long always on, shorts only in
     volatile) is not well-supported by the data. The val landscape
     inverts the stated rationale.

  3. Short-leg sizing is regime-dependent in a way that's hard to learn
     from one 2-year val window. Any principled production config
     should probably either (a) simplify to a constant small short size
     regardless of regime, or (b) use longer / walk-forward validation
     that captures multiple market regimes.

  Artifacts in results/mean_rev_pt3/ (Pt1/Pt2 preserved):
    mc_trials.csv         - 300 (sizing, metrics) rows
    mc_landscape.png      - scatter + histogram dashboard
    backtest_summary.png  - final test backtest dashboard
    best_sizing.txt       - winning triple + test metrics
    result.pkl            - full backtest state + mc_df for ICM

  Pt1 remains the best production candidate on the held-out test set.
  Pt3 provides the "why" evidence for the ICM's "conditions under which
  it loses money" and "robustness to parameter choice" sections.
