    Generalization loss    -0.108           -0.306  (proportionally)
    Sizing inversion         yes              yes
    Hand-picked wins test?   YES              YES

  Consistent story for the ICM:
    1. BOTH hand-picked sizings are suboptimal on validation — the MC
       easily finds better configurations.
    2. BOTH MC-optimal val sizings fail to generalize to test; the
       hand-picked sizings generalize better despite being "wrong" on val.
    3. Reversal sleeve's hand-picked sizing is MORE wrong on val (only
       0.3% of MC trials failed to beat it, vs 38.7% for momentum).
       Momentum's hand-picked sizing is closer to the val optimum.
    4. In both sleeves, the MC landscape suggests the opposite of the
       stated rationale: positions should be LARGER in volatile/crash
       regimes, SMALLER in trending regimes. This is unexpected.

  Likely explanation: 2020-2022 validation window contained COVID crash
  + tech rally + 2022 bear market onset — an unusual regime mix that
  rewarded contrarian sizing. 2022-2026 test contains AI bull + moderate
  rotation — closer to Pt2/Pt1's intended regime character. The
  "hand-picked wins test" result is regime-dependent coincidence,
  not principled robustness.

  ICM implication: the "regime-aware sizing" component of both strategies
  is the weakest link — not predictive, not robust, and largely arbitrary.
  Walk-forward validation across multiple market regimes would be needed
  to establish any principled sizing policy.

